From 0c0cd848559db01bc188480fc39a69c55468dfbb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Igor Velkov <325961+iav@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 23:54:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/49] rocket: vendor single-interrupt scheme; PPU max pooling

Completion now follows the vendor driver (rknpu_job_subcore_commit_pc):
only the LAST task's interrupt is unmasked, so a whole chained graph
completes with a single interrupt (mobilenet_v1: one IRQ for 27
operations, was ~27).  The mask is passed in the new last_int_mask job
field (kernel module updated in lockstep).

Max pooling lowers to a bare PPU/PPU_RDMA register chunk copied
verbatim from the vendor stream (resnet18 task 1, probe-MP): 29 words,
kernel/stride/pads in minus-one encoding, pad value 0x7ff80 (-128 as
19-bit), strides in bytes, started by a broadcast OP_EN 0x60 and linked
into the PC chain like any stream.

Two constraints found by replaying the captured vendor probe-MP submit
through rocket:

The PPU chunk must live in the SAME BO as the previous operation's
regcmd streams.  Moving it into its own BO wedges even the verbatim
vendor chain (the PC fetches convolution streams across BO boundaries
fine, a PPU chunk it does not); pool chunks are appended to the
producer's regcmd BO, the vendor layout.

POOLING_PADDING_CFG carries all four pads: [3:0] left, [7:4] top,
[11:8] right, [15:12] bottom.  Every pooling window must fall inside
input+padding on both axes or the PPU starves forever and the chain
wedges -- fixed by programming the right/bottom pads TFLite SAME
actually uses (top/left alone cannot express SAME).

resnet18 with the maxpool on the NPU merges into one partition:
39 -> 13 ms (CPU 345 ms), max diff 1, mean 0.32, top-5 unchanged, 10/10
runs stable.  RKT_NO_PPU=1 falls back to the CPU.
